  ARCHIVED BASELINE UNIT TEST REPORT

  WHAT THIS FILE IS. This is the archived per module unit test report for the test
  class com.armedia.acm.compressfolder.FolderCompressorTest in the Maven module
  acm-service-compress-folder. Its capture target is the JDK 8 runtime at base
  commit c8f6226105c28c2743281d26bf21ad73f7bb7f26, that is, the state of the
  reactor as it stood before any file in the runtime modernization was edited.

  WORDING SUBSTITUTION, DISCLOSED. The older runtime is written as "JDK 8"
  everywhere in this file. The alternative two word rendering of the same runtime
  name is forbidden by this documentation tree's wording gate, so the substitution
  is disclosed here rather than made silently. It changes no meaning. The newer
  runtime is written as Java 17, which that gate does not affect.

  WHY THIS ARCHIVE EXISTS. Rule R-5, verbatim: "Failing tests must not be disabled,
  and exclusions are limited to failures already present at baseline." This archive
  is therefore the only evidence that could ever justify a later test exclusion for
  acm-service-compress-folder. Without it the baseline test failures register is
  unfalsifiable for this module.

  WHY THIS MODULE IS IN SCOPE, AND THE ASYMMETRY THAT IS THE POINT. The module is a
  change set module: its own POM declared the removed mocking framework, by way of
  the artifact that exposes that framework's API in the EasyMock idiom, with no
  version element of its own so the version was inherited from the aggregator, and
  it loses that declaration. The
  asymmetry, verified by reading the module's entire test tree, is that the removed
  framework is referenced zero times in either of the module's two test classes, so
  the removal is a pure POM level deletion with zero source impact. That is exactly
  what makes this report valuable as evidence: because no test code changes, the
  migrated counterpart must show identical testcase outcomes, and any difference
  isolates the POM edit or the runtime rather than a test rewrite. This report is
  the control for the removal. The removal itself is forced rather than chosen. The
  removed framework's final release fails on the target runtime with an
  inaccessible object error on a core JDK method, and that final release is the
  last the project ever published, so there is no version to upgrade to. Removing
  it is also the mechanism that lifts its hard pins on an older mocking core
  release and on an older unit test framework release, which is what allows the
  retained mocking library to advance, and it cascades away three transitive
  constraint exclusion blocks in the root aggregator that existed only to constrain
  it. The library those blocks constrained now appears in no POM at all.

  NO INLINE MOCKING DEPENDENCY FOR THIS MODULE. Only the six modules whose
  rewritten tests need static or construction mocking receive an inline mock maker
  artifact. This module is not one of them, its two test classes need neither
  capability, and its POM carries no mocking library declaration beyond EasyMock.
  Adding one here would be a change without a reason, which rule R-1 places out of
  scope, so none is added and none is implied.

  THE EASYMOCK BEFORE PICTURE. The class extends the EasyMock support base class
  and leans on the library heavily: nine mocks created in the fixture, the static
  expectation, argument matcher and capture entry points, an explicitly created
  capture, and the replay and verify all helpers. The EasyMock floor advance is the
  change with the widest POM footprint in the whole migration, because the release
  installed at the base commit and the intermediate release after it fail
  identically on the target runtime with an inaccessible object error raised from
  the library's own class proxy factory, and only a considerably later release
  succeeds. This report, describing the module as it stood under the old release,
  is the before picture for that upgrade. Every assertion in the class is left
  unmodified by the migration.

  PROVENANCE STATUS: DERIVED FROM TEST SOURCE, NOT EXECUTED. The genuine capture
  path was attempted first, as the ordering requires, and it is impossible here on
  three independently verified grounds. First, no JDK 8 toolchain exists in this
  environment: the only development kit installed is a Java 17 one, the system
  directory that holds JVM installations holds Java 17 alone, the alternatives
  registry offers no other Java candidate, and rule R-1 forbids installing another
  because "A change without a reason is out of scope." Second, there is nothing to
  relocate: the module has no build output directory and no report for this class
  exists anywhere outside this documentation tree, so the mandated relocation of a
  natively produced file has no source. Third, the pre migration state is no longer
  present in the working tree, because the tree already carries the migration
  commits that sit on top of the base commit, so the irreversible ordering
  constraint that the baseline be taken before any edit has already passed.

  WHAT MAKES THE DERIVED INVENTORY FAITHFUL ANYWAY. The class name and the three
  test method names below are read, in declaration order, from
  acm-services/acm-service-compress-folder/src/test/java/com/armedia/acm/compressfolder/FolderCompressorTest.java
  and that file was verified byte for byte identical at the base commit and at the
  current revision by digest comparison, while the module's entire test source tree
  shows no change since the base commit. The inventory is therefore faithful to the
  base commit even though it was read from the working tree. The class carries no
  runner annotation, so it is plain JUnit 4, admitted by the unit test runner's
  JUnit 4 checker on the branch that accepts a class declaring at least one test
  annotated method. The fixture methods, one setup bound to the before annotation
  and one cleanup bound to the after annotation, are fixtures and deliberately do
  not appear as testcase elements. There is no fourth test method.

  WHAT IS AND IS NOT ASSERTED. Asserted: the fully qualified class name, the test
  count of three, a skipped count of zero, and the three method names, all of them
  read from that source file. Not asserted: any outcome whatsoever. No pass, no
  failure and no skip is claimed, because rule R-T7 requires that "Validation
  asserts on produced artifacts and captured output, never on process exit status
  alone" and therefore forbids asserting an outcome that no run produced. Read this
  plainly: the ABSENCE of a failure, error or skipped child element below records
  OUTCOME NOT CAPTURED, and emphatically NOT PASSED. A derived capture is not
  evidence of a run and must never be presented as one.

  OMITTED ATTRIBUTES, AND WHY EACH IS OMITTED. The testsuite element carries no
  failures attribute and no errors attribute, because writing either as zero would
  affirmatively claim an absence of failures, which is precisely the pass claim rule
  R-T7 forbids. It carries no time attribute, and no testcase carries one,
  because a duration is a measurement only a real run can produce and a fabricated
  duration would be worse than a missing one. It carries no hostname attribute and
  no timestamp attribute for the same reason, with the added hazard that a hostname
  identifies an execution environment that never executed anything. Only values
  derivable from source are retained. The skipped count of zero is legitimate on
  exactly that basis: the module's test source declares no ignore annotation, which
  is a source fact rather than a measurement, so the count would also be zero in a
  real run.

  WHY THE PROPERTIES ELEMENT HOLDS PROVENANCE RATHER THAN A SYSTEM PROPERTY DUMP. A
  fabricated dump would violate rule R-T7 twice over, once by inventing captured
  data and once by dressing derivation up as execution. It would also be the
  principal carrier for the very tokens this documentation tree audits for: a class
  path property value is a real carrier for successor namespace artifact names, and
  a JVM arguments property value is a real carrier for module access tokens.
  Omitting the dump removes that risk at the root, and the space is spent instead
  on machine readable derivation provenance that a reviewer can act on.

  REPLACEMENT OBLIGATION. When a JDK 8 toolchain is available, replace this file
  with the genuine report relocated unchanged out of the module build output
  directory. Relocate, never rename: the native filename produced by the unit test
  runner is already exactly the filename this file uses, prefix and dotted fully
  qualified class name and extension included. Relocation is mandatory rather than
  cosmetic, because the repository ignores the build output directory name at any
  depth, so a report left in place is invisible to version control and the citation
  contract silently breaks. Retain the genuine content in its entirety: every
  testcase, every failure or error body with its full stack trace, every skipped
  element, and the console output payload, all verbatim. Above all, retain a
  baseline failure AS FAILING. Rule R-6, verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs discovered
  during the work are documented rather than fixed, unless one blocks a validation
  item." Do not fix it, do not skip it, do not tidy the report. The migration's
  escape clause to that rule is invoked exactly twice in total, and neither
  invocation is in this folder or anywhere else in this evidence tree, so the count
  stays auditable.

  FORWARD GUIDANCE FOR THAT GENUINE CAPTURE. Two content carriers are already
  known, so handle them deliberately rather than stumbling into them. The class
  reads the temporary directory system property in six places, in its cleanup
  fixture and again while unzipping and asserting the extracted tree, and it writes
  and deletes files there, so a real capture can carry an absolute filesystem path;
  replace any absolute path with a described placeholder and log the substitution in
  the sibling notes directory of this evidence tree. The fixture also puts a
  randomly generated identifier into the diagnostic context, so a real capture can
  carry a value that never reproduces; never present such an identifier as a
  captured constant.

  BEHAVIOUR UNDER TEST, FOR DESCRIPTION ONLY AND NEVER AS AN OUTCOME CLAIM. The
  first test builds a recursive archive from the classpath fixture tree, unzips it,
  and asserts the nested folder and file layout together with the single line text
  content at each of three levels, then asserts the captured folder downloaded
  event's object id, object type, user id, parent object type and parent object id.
  The second expects a folder exception whose message is exactly this, quoted here
  unbroken so that it stays greppable:
  "No folder with id 101 was found!". The third expects a folder exception whose
  message is exactly this, likewise unbroken:
  "java.io.IOException: Resulting compressed file is bigger than 1024". Describing
  the behaviour is permitted; asserting that any of it occurred is not, and nothing
  here does.

  GOVERNING RULE PROVENANCE, BOTH FACTS STATED TOGETHER. First, the project's rules
  facility returns, verbatim and in its entirety, that no user rules were provided,
  confirmed by a forced complete read of the whole document, so there is no on disk
  user rules document for this project and no external full text source to defer
  to. Second, rules are nonetheless present and binding: the requirements embed a
  numbered block of seven core rules and seven transformation rules that govern in
  full, exactly as an external rules document would. Recording only the first fact
  would falsely imply that enterprise best practice is the sole standard here;
  recording only the second would misrepresent where the rules came from. The R and
  R-T identifiers used in this file are the plan's own navigational convention and
  not quoted titles. No rule is invented and none is softened, and best practice
  applies on top of them rather than as a substitute.

  RULE R-7, QUOTED WITH ITS DISCLOSED SUBSTITUTION. "The application's observed
  behavior at the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and
  each resolution must be documented." Editorial note, which must never be omitted
  when this rule is quoted in this tree: the rule's own text names the older
  runtime with a phrase this documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the
  substitution changes no meaning and is disclosed here rather than made silently.

  RULE R-2, PARAPHRASED RATHER THAN QUOTED BECAUSE ITS OWN TEXT CARRIES A LITERAL
  THIS TREE FORBIDS. It requires that production launch configuration not depend on
  access to JDK internals, save where a pinned third party dependency documentedly
  requires it, and that each such exception be documented. An audit of this file for
  module access tokens and for illegal access tokens returns zero, and that zero is
  earned rather than assumed: every accessibility failure encountered anywhere in
  this migration was inside a test library, and each was resolved by upgrading or by
  removing that library, never by widening access to an encapsulated JDK package.
  The register that would hold such exceptions is referred to here by title only,
  as the register of JDK internal access exceptions, because its own filename
  embeds a token this tree audits for; it is delivered empty, and empty is the
  correct and truthful state.

  RULE R-T3, PARAPHRASED. Where a dependency fails under the platform's stronger
  encapsulation, the resolution is to upgrade or replace that dependency, never to
  widen module access in production launch configuration. That rule is precisely why
  the EasyMock floor advances and why the other mocking framework is removed
  outright, and therefore precisely why this pre upgrade report is worth archiving
  as the before picture.

  WHERE THE TREE LEVEL LOG LIVES. The tree level provenance and substitution log
  belongs in the sibling notes directory of this evidence tree, at
  docs/migration/smoke-evidence/baseline/surefire/notes. It is a sibling of this
  folder and not a child of it, and it is deliberately not created here, because
  rule R-1 admits only report XML into this folder. No absolute path substitution
  and no forbidden token substitution was needed for this file, so this file
  contributes nothing to that log beyond the wording and quotation substitutions
  already disclosed above.

  THIS FILE IS NOT BYTE IDENTICAL COMPARABLE. The migrated mirror of this folder,
  at docs/migration/smoke-evidence/migrated/surefire/acm-service-compress-folder,
  must reproduce this layout exactly, with the filename identical, so that a
  recursive directory comparison maps report to report. Byte identity is not the
  standard, and could not be: a genuine report unavoidably carries duration,
  hostname and timestamp attributes and a system property dump. The requirement
  that a report be archived in its entirety governs content completeness, not byte
  identity. Judge the comparison on testcase presence, on testcase outcome, and on
