  reproducible and failing fast is safe. That library is an application dependency
  with nothing whatever to do with rerunning tests.

  FALSE POSITIVE TWO: COVERAGE EXCLUSIONS ARE NOT TEST EXCLUSIONS. The root
  aggregator configures the coverage plugin with exactly eight package glob
  exclusions. Those suppress coverage measurement only, they disable no test
  whatsoever, and not one of them matches this module's package. One related point
  binds anyone editing the build: the coverage agent goal communicates with the unit
  test runner through the default argument line property export, and the coverage
  report and threshold goals are both bound to the unit test phase with a minimum
  line coverage ratio of two hundredths. A newly added unit test runner declaration
  must therefore NOT set a literal argument line element, because doing so silently
  severs instrumentation and leaves the threshold goal evaluating an empty data set,
  which reports a false pass. Instrumentation must be verifiably live.
